  "14": {
    "name": "Integration",
    "main_text": "The conflict is inside you, and only there can it be resolved. Political opposition exists within you, playing out between the two hemispheres of your brain.\n\nA narrow passage connects these two parts of consciousness. In the event of an accident, a physiological deficiency or some other trauma, an individual's personality disintegrates and becomes schizophrenic.\n\nWhen the fragile passage between these two worlds breaks down, the personality splits and the individual behaves like two different people. You might be gentle and charming in the morning and furious by evening. You no longer remember the person you were that morning. You have become someone else, governed by another brain.\n\nWhen the ends of the two hemispheres are joined, the passage grows stronger and a unity takes shape. This is integration, or crystallisation.\n\n**What Gurdjieff called the crystallisation of being is the fusion of these two parts of the brain.**",
    "commentary": "The opposites in your life can now come together into a single whole.\n\nA tremendous energy will be released. Make the most of this opportunity now.\n\n---"
  },
  "15": {
    "name": "Conditioning",
    "main_text": "Until you let go of your personality, your â€œIâ€, you will not find your true individuality. **That individuality is the essence of your existence.**\n\nSociety imposes a personality on you and rejects the individual who refuses to follow the flock. The individual is like a lion, walking alone.\n\nSheep stay with the flock because it is comfortable and safe. Besides, should an enemy appear, a sheep stands a good chance of escaping notice among all the others. A lion takes the risk of standing out alone.",
    "commentary": "This card tells an old Zen story about a lion brought up by sheep. He believed he was a sheep too, until one day an old lion made him look at his reflection in a pond.\n\nMany of us are like that lion. The image we see of ourselves comes from other people's opinions of us rather than our own experience.\n\n**It is your turn to look into the mirror of the pond** and free yourself from what others have led you to believe.\n\n---"
  },
  "16": {
    "name": "Thunderbolt",
    "main_text": "What meditation brings about slowly can happen in an instant at the unexpected shout of a master.\n\nA disciple asks a question, and the master leaps up, shouts, strikes him or seizes him roughly. Such methods were unknown before Zen. They owe their origin to the creative genius of Ma Tzu, a master who guided many people towards awakening.\n\nIt could sometimes be funny. A man came to ask Ma Tzu how to meditate, and Ma Tzu threw him out of a second-storey window. Then he jumped out too, landed on the visitor's chest and shouted, â€œGot it?â€\n\nThe poor man answered yes for fear of what the master might do to him next. He had had enough. His bones were broken, and Ma Tzu was crushing his chest, yelling, â€œGot it?â€\n\nHe had no choice. And so he did grasp the lesson he had been given. **The teaching had been so unexpected, so abrupt!** He could never have imagined such a thing.",
    "commentary": "You may feel shaken just now, as if the earth were trembling beneath your feet. Your sense of security is threatened and you feel inclined to grab hold of anything you can.\n\nYet this inner earthquake is necessary and deeply significant. If you accept it, you will emerge from the wreckage stronger and ready for new experiences.\n\n---"
  },
  "17": {
    "name": "Silence",
    "main_text": "The energy of the whole has taken possession of you. You are possessed. You are no longer there. Only the One exists.\n\nThe moment silence settles within you, you understand its meaning. It is the same silence known to the Buddha, Chuang Tzu, Bodhidharma and Nansen. **The taste of silence is universal.**\n\nTime passes and the world changes constantly, yet the experience of silence and the joy it gives remain the same. It is the one thing you can count on, the one thing that never dies and that you can call your true being.",
    "commentary": "This time is precious. It will be easy for you to gather yourself, to sound the depths of your inner silence and find the point where it meets the silence of the universe.\n\nThere is nothing you need to do, nowhere you need to go. Your inner silence spreads into everything you do. **The time has come to turn inward again.**\n\n---"
  },
  "18": {
    "name": "Past Lives",
    "main_text": "A child can come into awareness only if, in a previous life, they devoted themselves to meditation and built up the meditative energy needed to face the darkness that comes with death.\n\nThe person who dies loses all awareness and suddenly finds themselves in a new womb, having entirely forgotten their previous body. The darkness, the loss of consciousness, creates a break.\n\nThe East has worked hard to open a way through these barriers. Ten thousand years of effort have borne fruit. Anyone can gain access to a past life, or to several.\n\nTo do this you must deepen your meditation. Without it, you will never find the door that opens onto another life. And when you do find that door, you must be deep in meditation, or what you encounter may overwhelm your mind. One life is already hard enough to manage.",
    "commentary": "A glimpse of the eternity of our existence is a gift. An understanding of how karma works in our lives is hardly something we can summon at will.\n\nIt is a call to awaken. The events of your life are trying to show you a pattern as old as the journey of your own soul.\n\n---"
  },
  "19": {
    "name": "Innocence",
    "main_text": "Zen says that if you let go of what you know, including your name, your identity and everything given to you from outside, your being will take on an entirely new quality, **the quality of innocence.**\n\nIt will be the crucifixion of your persona, your personality, and a resurrection of your innocence. You will be like a child again. You will be born anew.",
    "commentary": "The innocence that comes from a deep experience of life is childlike without being childish.\n\nA child's innocence is beautiful and unknowing. It gives way to mistrust and doubt as the child grows and discovers that the world can be threatening and dangerous.\n\nYet the innocence of a life fully lived has an air of wisdom about it. It accepts that life, forever changing, is a mystery.\n\n---"
  },
  "20": {
    "name": "Beyond Illusion",
    "main_text": "Why did people like the Buddha insist so strongly that all existence, apart from your inner witness, your awareness, is fleeting and made of the same stuff as dreams?\n\nThe Buddha is hardly claiming that trees do not exist. Take care with the word â€œillusionâ€. It is only an awkward translation of â€œmayaâ€.\n\nAn illusion has no existence, while reality does exist. Maya lies exactly between the two. It is a passing reality, something that almost exists.\n\nOur everyday life can be regarded as real. Yet in the ultimate sense, at the highest level of consciousness, **what was real becomes illusory and ceases to exist.**",
    "commentary": "This card invites us to turn our gaze inward and give up looking outside ourselves for reality.\n\nWhen we focus on what is outside, it is easy to get lost in judgements. â€œThis is good.â€ â€œThat is bad.â€ â€œI want this.â€ â€œI don't want that.â€ These opinions hold us captive in a world of illusion and old habits. We turn into robots, sleepwalkers.\n\nFree yourself from your opinionated mind and turn inward. Relax into the deep truth of your being, **where you have always known the difference between a dream and reality.**\n\n---"
  },
  "21": {
    "name": "Completion",
    "main_text": "It is typical of Zen to leave something unsaid. Try to understand this, for it is an important method.\n\nLeaving something unsaid gives your listener room to complete the situation for themselves. An answer is never absolute. The master only points in a direction. When you reach the limit, you will know what remains.\n\nIf you try to grasp Zen with the intellect, you will fail. Zen is less about receiving an answer to a question than about understanding something far greater.\n\nZen shows us reality. Your buddha-nature is near at hand. **Your very awareness is that nature.** And your awareness can witness everything that makes up the world. The world will go out, but the mirror will remain, reflecting emptiness.",
    "commentary": "Something that has occupied your time and energy until now is coming to an end.\n\nBringing it to completion opens the door to a new experience. Take this pause to celebrate both sides of this moment, the ending of what is complete and the arrival of something new.\n\n---"
  },
  "22": {
    "name": "The Master",
    "main_text": "Masters cannot teach the truth, because truth cannot be passed on. What the master teaches you goes further, beyond texts and words.\n\nIt is an energy the master stirs in you, a kind of synchronicity. You must approach a master with great love, complete trust and an open heart.\n\nYou do not know who you are. The master knows who he is **and** who you are. Like the caterpillar that has no idea it can become a butterfly, you are that caterpillar, a bodhisattva.\n\nAll caterpillars become bodhisattvas, and all bodhisattvas were once caterpillars. Bodhisattvas can turn into butterflies, and the butterfly in turn can become a buddha. It carries that potential within, for **it is a buddha in essence.**",
